This implementation defines a custom React hook called `useCrossTabAuth` that synchronizes authentication state across browser tabs. On mount, it reads persisted user data from `localStorage` and updates the Redux store. It also listens to the `storage` event to react to changes made in other tabs, such as login or logout. The hook exposes `handleLogin` and `handleLogout` helpers to centralize auth logic, ensuring Redux and `localStorage` stay in sync.

```js
import { useEffect } from "react";
import { useDispatch } from "react-redux";
import {
  resetUserLoggedIn,
  setUserData,
} from "../components/Modules/appReducer";

export const useCrossTabAuth = () => {
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  useEffect(() => {
    // Sync on mount
    const userData = localStorage.getItem("userData");
    if (userData) {
      dispatch(setUserData(JSON.parse(userData)));
    }

    // Listen for storage changes
    const handleStorageChange = (e) => {
      if (e.key === "userData") {
        if (e.newValue) {
          dispatch(setUserData(JSON.parse(e.newValue)));
        } else {
          dispatch(resetUserLoggedIn());
        }
      }
    };

    window.addEventListener("storage", handleStorageChange);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener("storage", handleStorageChange);
    };
  }, [dispatch]);

  const handleLogin = (
    userData,
    access_token,
    refresh_token
  ) => {
    const user = {
      ...userData,
      is_sso_user: userData.is_sso_user ?? true,
    };
    localStorage.setItem("token", access_token);
    localStorage.setItem("refresh_token", refresh_token);
    dispatch(setUserData(user));
    localStorage.setItem("userData", JSON.stringify(user));
    localStorage.setItem("isAnnonymousUser", "false");
  };

  const handleLogout = () => {
    dispatch(resetUserLoggedIn());
    localStorage.removeItem("userData");
    localStorage.removeItem("refresh_token");
    localStorage.removeItem("isAnnonymousUser");
    localStorage.removeItem("token");
  };

  return { handleLogin, handleLogout };
};
```

NGINX is commonly used as a reverse proxy in Docker-based applications to route incoming traffic to multiple services. In this setup, NGINX listens on port 80 inside the container and forwards requests to separate frontend and backend services running on the same Docker network.

The `/api/` path is routed to a backend service, while all other requests are forwarded to a frontend service (typically a SPA such as React or Angular).

```nginx
server {
    listen 80; # NGINX listens on port 80 inside the container
    server_name localhost;

    # Backend / API routing
    location /api/ {
        # Proxy requests to the backend service on the Docker network
        proxy_pass http://opik-backend:8080/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

    # Frontend routing (all other requests)
    location / {
        # Proxy requests to the frontend service on the Docker network
        proxy_pass http://opik-frontend:80;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;

        # Support client-side routing for SPAs
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }
}
```

Key Notes:
- Service names (`opik-backend`, `opik-frontend`) come from Docker Compose and act as internal DNS hostnames.
- NGINX runs inside its own container and communicates with other containers via the Docker network.
- The `try_files` directive ensures proper routing for single-page applications.
- Forwarded headers preserve the original client request information.
